  "generated_text": "1. First, calculate the distance Joann traveled.\n   - Joann's average speed is 12 miles per hour.\n   - Joann rode for 3.5 hours.\n   - Distance = Speed × Time\n   - Distance = 12 miles per hour × 3.5 hours\n   - Distance = 12 × 3.5\n   - Distance = 42 miles\n\n2. Now, we need to find the average speed Fran would need to travel the same distance in 3 hours.\n   - Fran rides for 3 hours.\n   - Distance Fran needs to travel is 42 miles.\n   - Speed = Distance / Time\n   - Speed = 42 miles / 3 hours\n   - Speed = 14 miles per hour\n\nTherefore, Fran would have to ride her bike at an average speed of \\(\\boxed{14}\\) miles per hour to travel the same distance that Joann traveled.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[14, '14']",
    "gold_answer": "In $3\\frac{1}{2}$ hours, Joann covers $\\left(3\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ hours}\\right)(12\\text{ mph})=42$ miles.  If Fran's average speed in miles per hour is $s$, then Fran covers $3s$ miles in $3$ hours.  Solving $3s=42$ we find $s=\\boxed{14}$ miles per hour.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
